  └────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

  What's straining the laptop isn't compute — it's memory. You never know which expert gets picked
  per token, so all 512 of them have to stay resident. Only 3B is actually running, but it occupies 80B's worth of space.

  So what I said earlier — that "34B Q4 might be better" — doesn't really apply to this model.
  That would hold for an 80B@Q2 dense model, but MoE has a big speed edge and its quality tracks the 80B side, so the original call was reasonable.

  So the only advice left is one thing

  Cut the context. This model's native context is 262,144 tokens (up to 1.01M extended). Leave it at
  the default and the KV cache balloons enormously.

  Fortunately this model was designed with few KV heads (16 Q : 2 KV, GQA). Even so, holding 260k tokens
  still costs gigabytes on its own. Dropping to 8k-16k keeps the weights the same but the pressure drops
  sharply. That's plenty for reading code or having a conversation.

  Summary

  - Laptop struggling = memory pressure (512 resident experts), not compute load
  - Top fix = shrink the context. Cheapest and most effective
  - Disk offloading? Still unnecessary. It's already loaded in memory, no reason to push it down
  - Only worry if memory pressure goes into the red

  Sources:
  - Model card

✻ Churned for 42s

※ recap: Diagnosed the temperature issue and wrote a new study note. We're now talking about the local LLM,
  and the next step is lowering the context to 8k-16k to ease the memory pressure.

